!Matinee - a Plex, Jellyfin and DLNA client for RISC OS     0.1.0
===================================================================

Matinee browses the films and TV programmes on a Plex Media Server (or
a Jellyfin server, or a DLNA media server) and plays them in its own window, with the player core of riscos-ffmpeg's
Reel (or hands them to ReelEGL or Reel, if you'd rather). Files it can
play as they are are played directly. Everything else is converted by
the server, as it plays, into a stream it can handle: H.264 video with
stereo AAC sound.

This is the first release. Please report problems at
https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-matinee/issues

Matinee was called PlexRO in its first test builds (its choices and
sign-in carry over). Plex is a trademark of Plex, Inc.; Matinee isn't
made by Plex or connected with it, nor with the Jellyfin project.

Starting
--------
Double-click !Matinee: its icon (a screen with posters and a play button)
appears on the icon bar. The first time, click Select on it to sign in.

Signing in
----------
Matinee has one window. Until you've signed in, it shows Add a server:
choose Plex, Jellyfin or DLNA (below). Plex's sign-in page has a four-letter
code. On any computer or phone, go to plex.tv/link,
sign in to your Plex account there, and type the code. Matinee checks every
2 seconds, and carries on by itself once the code has been typed: it finds
your servers and shows your libraries. Matinee never sees your password. A
code runs out after a while: New code gets another.

A server on your own network can be used without plex.tv: click in
Address and type its address (such as 192.168.1.10; :32400 is added),
then Tab (or Return) to Token and type a token (the server's or your
account's X-Plex-Token; it's shown as dots), then Return or Use these.
Backspace deletes, Ctrl-U empties the field.

Add a server... (on the icon's menu, and at the foot of Servers) asks
which kind the server is, Plex, Jellyfin or DLNA, then shows its sign-in.
Back goes to that choice again; Cancel (or Escape) adds nothing and goes
back to where you were.

Jellyfin servers
----------------
Matinee uses one server at a time: a Plex server or a Jellyfin server.
Servers on the icon's menu lists your Plex servers, then the Jellyfin
servers you've signed in to (ticked: the one in use), then Add a
server...; choose one to switch to it.

To sign in to a Jellyfin server, type its address (such as
192.168.1.20; :8096 is added unless you give a port or an http://
address), then either:
  Quick Connect  a six-digit code: in Jellyfin on a phone or computer,
                 open your user menu, choose Quick Connect and type it.
                 Matinee checks every 2 seconds and carries on by itself.
                 (The server's administrator may have turned it off.)
  your name and password, then Return or Sign in.
Matinee keeps the server's token, not your password.

On a Jellyfin server everything works as on a Plex one, except: there
are no star ratings (Jellyfin doesn't keep them), no Plex Home people,
and your choice of subtitles and sound track lasts while Matinee runs
(Jellyfin doesn't keep it). Skip intro and Skip credits appear where the
server has found them (Jellyfin 10.10 or later, with its media segments
plug-ins). Collections are the server's, not each library's. Remove from
Continue watching forgets where you got to.

Sign out of this server (the icon's menu, while a Jellyfin server is in
use) signs out on the server and forgets it; your Plex sign-in is kept.

DLNA servers
------------
A DLNA (UPnP) media server shares the files in its folders: MiniDLNA
(ReadyMedia, on many NAS boxes), a NAS's own media server, or the DLNA
side of Plex or Jellyfin. Choose DLNA under Add a server: Matinee looks
on your network for 2 seconds and lists the servers it finds; click one
to use it. Search again looks again. If yours isn't found (some routers
and Wi-Fi access points don't pass on the look), type its address, such
as 192.168.1.10:8200 (MiniDLNA) or 192.168.1.10:32469 (Plex's DLNA), or
the address of its description (http://.../rootDesc.xml), and press
Return or Use. There's nothing to sign in to.

Its top-level folders are the libraries (Music and Pictures are left
out); folders open as lists, a page at a time as other libraries do. Songs
and pictures are shown but not played. Search asks the server, if it can
search.

A DLNA server seldom says how a file is encoded, so Matinee goes by its
type, picture size and bit rate. A file that should play is played as it
is. Otherwise, if the server offers converted versions (Plex's DLNA,
Serviio and Universal Media Server do; MiniDLNA doesn't), the biggest
that fits the Quality is played; if not, the file is tried anyway. A
4K file on a Pi 4 is taken to be HEVC, for its HEVC block. Seeking in a
converted stream depends on the server.

DLNA servers don't keep where you got to or what you've watched, so
Matinee keeps them, in Choices (Matinee.Places): Continue watching on the
home page, Resume, the tick when watched, and Mark watched work as on a
Plex server. There are no star ratings, subtitle or sound track choices,
or Skip intro. Forget this server (the icon's menu) forgets the server;
where you got to in its videos is kept, for if it's added again.

The browser
-----------
Select on the icon bar icon opens it: a dark window, like a TV's. At the
top: Back, where you are, a status line, Search and Refresh; under them,
tabs for Home and each of your libraries, on every page, so a library is
one click away (the one you're in is light; Back from a library goes
Home). Below them, a
poster for each film, show, season or episode, with its name and (for
example) its year, "S1 E3" or "3 seasons". On the posters: a bar along
the foot shows how far you got; a tick in a circle, that you've watched
it; on a show or series, a blue number, how many episodes you haven't.
The selected poster has a light border, and the one under the pointer a
grey one.

  click          select
  Menu, or Adjust (a PC mouse's right button): the poster's menu (below)
  double-click   open a library, show or season (a show: its page, below);
                 a video's details page
  / or Ctrl-F    search (or the magnifying glass in the bar)
  Return         open, or play, the poster that's selected
  I              the selected video's details page
  arrow keys     move the selection
  Backspace, Escape, Delete  go back

A library has a bar over its posters: Library (its films or shows),
Collections and Playlists (your video playlists); for Library, Sort (by
Title, Date added, Year or Rating, newest or best first) and Unwatched
(only what you haven't seen); and, sorted by title, A to Z: click a
letter to go to the first title starting with it (grey letters have
none). Back and Refresh keep what you chose; the library's tab sets it
back to all, by title.

A big library comes 2000 at a time: the first 2000 straight away, then the
rest while you look (the status line says "2000 of 5400: the rest
coming."). A to Z's letters light up as their titles arrive.

Poster size on the icon's menu: Small, Medium (the default) or Large,
remembered.

The home page
-------------
The top is a home page. Across it, the first thing in Continue watching,
featured: its backdrop fading into the page, its title, labels, two
lines of its summary, Resume (or Play) with how far you got and how long
is left, and Details; the dots on the right feature one of the next few
instead. Under it, rows: Continue watching (pictures with a bar for how
far you got), Recently added in each film and TV library (posters), and
(your libraries are the tabs under the bar). A row shows as many as fit the window; click its title
(with the arrow) for the whole list. A series in Recently added opens its
show's page. Films and TV libraries open; music and photo libraries
aren't handled yet.

  Left, Right    along a row
  Up, Down       the row above or below
  Return         open or play what's selected; I its details

Menu on a poster:
  Play (Open)         play it (open a folder)
  Details...          its details page (below)
  Resume from h:mm:ss where Plex says you stopped. The server streams it
                      from there (copying the video when it can)
  Play from start     from the beginning
  Subtitles >         None, or one of its subtitle tracks (below)
  Save original file > the file itself, onto your disc (below)
  Test speed          how fast the server sends it (below)
  Mark watched, Mark unwatched (a show or a series: all its episodes)
  Rate >              No rating, or 1 to 5 stars: yours, kept by the
                      server (the Plex apps show it too)
  Remove from Continue watching   off the home page's first row (where
                      you got to is kept)
  Back, Refresh

Searching
---------
The magnifying glass in the bar (or / or Ctrl-F) opens the search: type,
and a moment after you stop the films, shows and episodes that match
appear, from all your libraries (films first, then shows, then
episodes). Backspace deletes, Ctrl-U empties the field; Return opens or
plays the poster that's selected, as elsewhere. Back (or Escape) leaves
the search; from something you opened from it, Back returns to the
results.

The details page
----------------
Double-click a film or episode (or press I) and the window shows its
details. Across the top, its backdrop (the window's width and up to about
half its height), fading into the page; over it on the left, its poster
(an episode: its show's), which grows and shrinks with the window.
Beside the poster: the title; labels for the year, running time, age
rating and scores; its genres; the summary; the buttons (Play, Resume
from h:mm:ss, From the start, Mark watched or unwatched, Subtitles, Save
file); Your rating (five stars: click one to rate it, the one you
gave again to take the rating away); and how it will play. Under them: the cast, with their photos and
the parts they play (the photos arrive a moment after the page), and
beside the cast (under it in a narrow window) who directed and wrote it,
the studio, when it came out (or was first shown) and the file (such as
"1080p H.264 . AAC 5.1 . MKV . 4.2 GB"). Under those: the collection
it's in, with the other films in it (on a Plex server; click one for its
details), More like this (a film's: click one for its details) and
Trailers and extras (click one to play it).

  Return         play
  Left, Right    the video before or after it in the list
  Back, Backspace, Escape  back to the posters, where you were

A show's page
-------------
A show opens as a page of its own: its backdrop, poster, title, labels
(the year it began, how many series, the age rating, the score and how
many episodes you haven't watched), its summary, and two buttons: Play
(or Resume) the next episode to watch, and Mark watched (the whole show).
Under them, its series as tabs, the one with episodes you haven't seen
chosen, and that series' episodes as a list: a picture, the title, how
long it is (and how much is left), and two lines of its summary, with
the bar and the tick as on the posters. The first episode you haven't
finished is selected. Click a tab for another series.

  click, arrow keys       select an episode
  double-click, I         its details page
  Return                  play it
  Back, Backspace, Escape where you opened the show from

Subtitles
---------
Choose them on the Subtitles button on the details page, Subtitles on a
poster's menu, or while it plays: Subtitles on the bar under the picture,
or on the menu over the picture. None turns them off; or one of the
video's tracks (in the file, or a file of their own beside it on the
server). The choice is kept on the server, so the Plex apps on your
other devices show the same.

The built-in player draws them itself, playing the file directly: text
(SubRip, ASS, WebVTT, MP4 text) and pictures (DVD, Blu-ray, DVB) in the
file, and text files beside it. Changing them while it plays is at once.
Otherwise the server burns them into the picture: with ReelEGL or Reel
(which can't be told which to show), for a video the server converts
anyway, and for picture subtitles in a file of their own. Changing them
then starts the server's conversion again from where you are.

Playing
-------
Player on the icon's menu chooses what plays the video: Built-in (the
default), ReelEGL or Reel.

Built-in: the video plays in Matinee's window, at the size the window is
(the window opens at three quarters of the screen, in the middle). Play carries on from where you stopped (as Resume); From
the start (or Play from start) begins again. Under the picture: the title and the time, Back, Play or
Pause, back and on 10 seconds, the position bar (click to go there),
Subtitles (when it has some), Stats and Full screen.

  Space          pause, play
  Left, Right    back, on 10 seconds
  Down, Up       back, on a minute
  F              full screen, or back
  M              the mini player, or back (below)
  S              the stats (as YouTube's "Stats for nerds"), drawn in
                 the picture: the connection's speed, how much is read
                 ahead, the time decoding takes, and so on
  +, -           louder, quieter
  Return         Skip intro or Skip credits, while it shows
  Page Down, Page Up  the next chapter; this one's start (or the one
                 before, near its start)
  Escape         out of full screen or the mini player; else back to
                 where you were
  Backspace      back
  click          pause, play; double-click: full screen

Full screen is a window over the whole screen, still in the desktop:
other programs carry on. Move the pointer to see the controls; a pointer
left still over the picture for 2 seconds is hidden (*Set
Matinee$NoHidePointer 1 to keep it).

The stats (S) are drawn in the picture at a size you can read, in a
window or full screen: the connection's speed, how much has come in and
how much is read ahead (with graphs), the codecs, who decodes the picture
(Decoder: the VideoCore, the HEVC block or the ARM), the timing, and what's left out to
keep up (Keeping Up), as in ReelEGL.

On a Raspberry Pi, H.264 (most files, and everything the server
converts) is decoded by the Pi's VideoCore, as in Reel 0.1.23, leaving
the ARM free: up to 1920x1088, 8-bit 4:2:0; 1080p needs gpu_mem of 128
MB or more (in config.txt). Anything else, or a machine without the
VideoCore, is decoded on the ARM as before.

On a Raspberry Pi 4 (and 400), HEVC (H.265) is decoded by its HEVC
block: Main and Main 10 (10-bit), 4:2:0, up to 4096 wide (4K at up to
30 a second), and up to 40 Mbit/s whatever the Quality, so HEVC files
are played as they are instead of being converted by the server. The
block's pictures are converted for the screen on the ARM, so 4K keeps
it busy. *Set Matinee$NoHEVCBlock 1 before starting Matinee turns it
off (HEVC is then converted by the server again). The built-in player
only.

Menu over the picture: Sound track, Subtitles, Chapters, Volume, Picture
(Fit, Fill (crop) or Stretch), Stats, Full screen, Mini player, Keep on
top, Hardware overlay, Stop.

Skip intro and Skip credits: where the server has found a programme's
opening titles or end credits (Plex Pass: the library's intro and
credits detection), a button at the right of the bar's top line skips
them (Return too; in full screen the bar stays while it's there). Credits
that run to the end skip to the end: watched, and the next episode
offered. Chapters: the file's (as the server lists them) are on the
menu, by the time they start, and show as gaps in the position bar.

The mini player (M, or Mini player on the menu), as in Reel: the window
gives way to a small one with no title bar in the bottom right of the
screen, just above the icon bar: the picture (320 pixels wide at first),
Play/Pause, the position bar and Normal. Drag the picture to move it,
and the grip in its bottom right corner to make it bigger or smaller (it
keeps the video's shape); it opens there, at that size, next time.
Double-click the picture, click Normal, press M or Escape, or click
Matinee's icon, for the window again. The next episode plays on in it
(the Up next card isn't shown; it starts after 10 seconds).

It decodes a little less: the deblocking filter is skipped on the
pictures nothing else is predicted from (about 15% less work, and
nothing you can see at that size), and back on in the window.

Keep on top: RISC OS has no "always on top" for ordinary windows, so
with it ticked the mini player looks about once a second while playing
and, if another window has been opened over it, comes back to the
front. It never takes the caret from what you're typing in.

Hardware overlay (on by default): the picture goes to the display
through the VideoOverlay module, which scales it to the window or the
screen. Stretching it costs almost nothing, so full screen is as quick
as a small window. Where there's no overlay (the module is missing, or
the screen mode is too big), or while a menu or a window is over the
picture, it's drawn the ordinary way instead.

The server is told where you've got to as it plays, so Continue
watching and Resume work in the Plex apps on your other devices too,
and it shows in the server's dashboard under Now Playing (as the
computer's network name, Inet$HostName, if it has one). At
the end of an episode the next one is offered, and starts in 10 seconds
(Return: now; Escape: back).

Seeking in a video the server is converting works as in a file: the
server starts converting from the new place, which can take a few
seconds. While one is paused, Matinee tells the server now and then that
it's still wanted.

A log of what the player did can go to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.MatineeLog: it's
off unless you *Set Matinee$Log on (or to a file's name) before starting
Matinee. Send it with a report of a problem while playing.

ReelEGL or Reel: if it's running, it gets the video at once; if not,
Matinee starts it. The Filer must have seen the player's application
(open the directory it's in, or add it to your boot sequence), or
Matinee can't find it.

The status line says how it will play:
  "Direct Play: ..."   the file itself: nothing is changed by the server
  "Transcoded (...)"   the server converts it, and says why (for example
                       HEVC video, 10-bit video, or a bit rate above the
                       quality's limit)
The stats (S) say the same on their first line.

Quality on the icon's menu sets the limits, for direct play and for
what the server converts to: 1080p (10 Mbit/s), 720p (4 Mbit/s, the
default) or 480p (2 Mbit/s). The Raspberry Pi 4 decodes H.264 on one
core, so the bit rate matters as much as the size. Untick Direct play
when possible to have the server convert everything.

If a video keeps stopping to buffer
-----------------------------------
Test speed, on a video's menu, reads the start of its file from the
server for 8 seconds (the desktop carries on meanwhile), then says how
fast it came, in Mbit/s, and how much the file needs:
  plenty           the server and the network keep up: the player isn't
                   reading fast enough (tell us, with the numbers)
  too slow         untick Direct play (the server then converts it to
                   the Quality's limit), choose a lower Quality, or save
                   the file first and play it from the disc
It also says whether the server is reached over http or https (https is
slower on RISC OS), and whether it's on the local network.

The built-in player reads ahead up to 10 seconds. RISC OS gives the
reading only the time Matinee itself has, so while the read-ahead is
under 6 seconds Matinee doesn't sleep between pictures: it gives that
time to the reading until it's back to 9.5 seconds (the Task Manager
shows Matinee busier meanwhile). With the log on (above), every 10
seconds MatineeLog says how far ahead it is and how fast it came: send
it with a report of a video that buffers.

Saving the original file
------------------------
Move to the right of Save original file on a poster's menu: a save box
opens. Drag its icon to a directory display (or type a full path and
click Save). The file is saved a piece at a time, so the desktop carries
on; the status line shows how far it's got, and how fast (MB/s). Stop saving is on the same
menu while it's saving.

RISC OS's filing systems hold files of up to 4GB, so bigger files are
refused at once.

The image cache
---------------
The posters, backdrops and cast photos the server sends are kept on the
disc, in Choices (Matinee.Cache), so lists and details pages come up
quicker the next time. It holds up to 64 MB (image_cache_mb in the
Choices file; 0 turns it off): past that the oldest go when Matinee
starts. Clear image cache on the icon's menu empties it, and says how
big it was.

Sign out (on the icon's menu) asks first. With a Plex server it forgets
your Plex sign-in; with a Jellyfin server, that server only; with a DLNA
server it's Forget this server.

Forget a server (at the foot of Servers) lists every server Matinee
keeps: your Plex sign-in (or a Plex server given by hand), each Jellyfin
server and each DLNA server, whether or not it's the one in use. Choose
one and Matinee asks first; only if you say OK is it forgotten (a
Jellyfin server is signed out of too; where you got to on a DLNA server
is kept). Forgetting the server in use closes the window, as Sign out.

Who's watching (Plex Home)
--------------------------
If your Plex account is a Plex Home, Switch user on the icon's menu
lists its people; the one watching is ticked, and the window's title
says who it is. Choose someone to switch to them: their Continue
watching, watched marks, ratings and restrictions. Someone with a PIN
has "(PIN)" after their name: move to the right of it, type the PIN in
the box and press Return. Matinee remembers who was watching for the
next time.

Choices
-------
Kept in Choices:Matinee.Choices: the player, the quality, direct play,
the poster size, the built-in player's picture, volume and hardware
overlay, the server in use, who's watching (Plex Home), the Jellyfin
servers you've signed in to, the DLNA servers you've used, and the tokens that sign Matinee in to
plex.tv and the servers. Anyone who can read your Choices can use those tokens: Sign out
on the icon's menu removes them.

Needs
-----
- RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine (Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/400 and so on).
- For sound, SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager (the built-in player,
  ReelEGL and Reel all use them):
  download ssb.zip from https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
  and merge its !System. John Duffell's own site (now on the Internet
  Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
- SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (PackMan).
- AcornSSL (RISC OS 5.28 or later) for signing in with a code, and for
  servers reached through their plex.direct addresses.
- VideoOverlay (in !System on RISC OS 5), for the hardware overlay.
- On a Raspberry Pi, the VCHIQ module (in RISC OS 5's ROM) for decoding
  on the VideoCore; on a Pi 4, nothing more for its HEVC block.
- ReelEGL or Reel 0.1.17 or later (riscos-ffmpeg), only to play in them.

!Run loads the modules it needs. PThreadTicker 0.03 (UnixLib's thread
timer) is in the application directory. Matinee is built with UnixLib
5.0.3.3 (riscos-unixlib) and FFmpeg 5.1.10-riscos20 with Reel 0.1.27's
player core (riscos-ffmpeg).

If you used an earlier test build, Matinee starts with the built-in
player once; choose ReelEGL on the Player menu to go back to it.

Debugging: *Set Matinee$Debug 1 before starting it lets FFmpeg's network
messages through (to see them, run !RunImage from a TaskWindow).

